computing/INSTALL

These instructions create and install a copy of Theory of Computing.   Note
that I ran these on a spare laptop, so these have been tested.

These are written for Linux because that is what I have.  I expect they
also work on a Mac.  However, if you have Windows then I'm sorry, I cannot
help you.  I am not a Windows-knowledgable person, sorry.

Preliminary Comment: there has been an issue between Asymptote and Ghostscript.
  There was a vulnerability in GS and they made some changes, and then the
ASY version did not work with the GS version.  Consequently, in 2021
I put a bunch of .pdf's such as src/background/asy/3d/3d000.pdf into the
repo.  I am trying to return to generating them from source in the
Makefile but it is a process.

Step 0) Fresh Ubuntu 20 install, from scratch.  Fresh TeX Live, from the apt
package texlive-full.  Also install git, make, Ghostscript, Python, and Racket.

1) Clone the book's git repo.  In a terminal do this.
  $ cd ~/Documents
  $ git clone https://gitlab.com/jim.hefferon/toc.git computing
(You can call the top-level directory either 'computing' or 'toc' or
'toc-master', but 'computing' is best because it is what I use so it is
the most tested.  Thus your tree of files will look like computing/src/... and
computing/bin/... etc.  Other names will cause my Asymptote files to fail to
compile, sorry.)

2) Set up Asymptote environment.
  a) Make sure that imagemagick is installed.  In
      /etc/ImageMagick-6/policy.xml
    change lines at the bottom about PS, PDF, etc
    from rights="none" to rights="read|write".
  b) Put the following lines at the end of your .profile in your home
  directory (change "/Documents/computing" to wherever you put the repo).

# Directories for the Asymptote drawing program
export ASYMPTOTE_DIR=$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy:$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy/share:$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy/asy-graphtheory-master/modules:$ASYMPTOTE_DIR

  Log out and log in again (this runs the profile).  You can check that the
  prior works by running "echo $ASYMPTOTE_DIR" in a terminal.

3) Make sure you have a late enough Asymptote.
  a) Change to the 3d directory and try to run it.
    $ cd ~/Documents/computing
    $ cd src/background/asy/3d
    $ asy 3d
  If it compiles into 3d000.pdf without comment then you are all set.
  If you get an error about a stack then you need to recompile Asymptote;
  I needed to recompile when I installed on Ubuntu 20 in 2022.
    $ sudo apt-get build-dep asymptote
    $ cd ~/Downloads
    $ git clone https://github.com/vectorgraphics/asymptote asymptote
    $ cd asymptote
    $ ./autorun.sh
    $ ./make
    $ sudo make install
  (Then I had to close the terminal and open a new one for the new version
  to come up.)  By the way, just to be sure, I recompiled Ghostscript
  from its git repo in the same way.

  (Running on Ubuntu 22 did not require recompiling Asymptote or Ghostscript.)
  
3) Make the book.
  $ cd ~/Documents/computing/src
  $ make 2>&1 | tee Makefile.log
Then wait.  It can take a long time, ten minutes or more.  At the end you
have book.pdf and answers.pdf.

The command line "2>&1" stuff gives you a .log file to examine
if there is some trouble.  Sometimes I find that I need more packages from
TeX Live.  The other thing that can happen is that sometimes you get a
footnote "counter too large" message and a question mark prompt.  In this case
type x and hit Enter, and then "pdflatex book" should work; what has happened
is that on the first run LaTeX does not know that footnotes are per-page
and it can run out of footnotes in the counter but on the second run it is OK.

You can get the slides as here.
  $ make slides
  If you get told that background05.pdf is not there, run
"touch slides/background/asy/background.asy" and
"make slides/background/asy/background00.pdf" amd then "make slides" again.
I don't understand this bug.
 


